A||||6||David Gamache||anyone rebuilt an 87-88 rack and pinion||||5||
Z||000000||David Gamache||02-17-2000||07:27 PM||||I have not yet taken a rack apart yet on the 87 or 88, but i understand the bushings wear out and I am not sure what else usually wears. I have consulted my factory service manual but am still a little leary to start taking things apart. Has anyone had experence disassembling these units and if so what is the best method of repair.<BR>Thanks. ||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||02-17-2000||07:50 PM||||An 87 or an 88???? it matters. 87's use the same racks as all others. 88's are 88 only and have diferant problems.||||reg||1
Z||000002||David Gamache||02-17-2000||08:01 PM||||I have both 87 and 88 model years. You are correct, they are completely different units.||||reg||1
Z||000003||mwbackus||02-17-2000||11:02 PM||||You can get aluminum rack bushings for either year from Rodney Dickman:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.execpc.com/~rodney1/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.execpc.com/~rodney1/</A> <P><BR>Mike<P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||DRH||02-18-2000||06:58 PM||||David,<P>I took mine apart on my 88 Formula to replace the passenger side bushing. I used the plastic one from Pontiac because I didn't know about the aluminum one mentioned above at the time. The rest of mine seemed in pretty good shape after cleaning up. I did not replace anything else. <P>The biggest headache was getting the whole assembly out of the car. It slides right out, but only if you twist just so!<P>The only real problem I had with disassembly was loosening the lock nut on the cap that holds the spring that holds the tension on the rack (hows that for a part name???). It was steel and had become permanently connected to the aluminum housing. I had to heat with a torch to get it loose.<P>The only other part I remember that might be tricky was aligning the pinion gear with the rack. Not really that hard if you have the manual. It was very precise, the pinion shaft has to be aligned just so with the housing when the rack is a certain measurement from the end.<P>I had never done one before and did not find it that difficult. Maybe the equivalent or rebuilding the rear calipers???<P>David.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Rodney||02-18-2000||09:38 PM||||You do not need to remove the rack to replace the passenger side bushing on any year Fiero steering rack. It can be done in the car. Nice to take one apart to check everything but no need in most cases. <P>Rodney Dickman||||reg||1
Z||000006||Ozzy||02-19-2000||02:54 AM||||Ditto with Rodney (Hey Dude) I just bought the aluminum replacement from Rodney here. Have not got it yet but I'm sure it will be just fine. After looking closely at my rack, there is no slop in the pinion assy. and the drivers side is tight. <P>I was going to have a replacement made myself at a machine shop from high density plastic. When you look at the OEM bushing, it is any wonder it does not last. The plastic carrier is like really light for the beating it takes.<P>Ozzy||||reg||1
